# Project Structure: how the files connect

This guide shows what each file in the project does, which files use which, and how data moves from `tasks.csv` to `comparison.csv`. For details on one file, follow the links to `Data_Validation.md` and `Scheduling_Model.md`.

## 1. Overview

The project plans a small onboarding pilot with 6 tasks (T01 to T06) and 2 workers. The goal is the fewest working days to finish all tasks. The best possible answer is 11 days.

| Phase | What it builds | Status in the folder |
|---|---|---|
| Phase 1 | Reads and checks `tasks.csv`, and models the schedule as states and moves | Files exist, 26 tests pass |
| Phase 2 | Four search algorithms (BFS, UCS, Greedy, A*) that use the Phase 1 model | Files exist (see section 8) |
| Phase 3 | Better heuristics, as described in `Phase2_Requirements.md` section 8 | Not started in code. `heuristics.py` already holds some candidates |

## 2. How the files connect

An arrow means "is used by" (the file at the tail is imported or read by the file at the head). Data files are the slanted boxes.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph Data
        tasks[/tasks.csv/]
        ref[/reference_schedule.csv/]
        out[/comparison.csv/]
    end
    subgraph P1[Phase 1]
        dv[data_validation.py]
        sm[scheduling_model.py]
        t1[test_phase1.py]
    end
    subgraph P2[Phase 2]
        sc[search_common.py]
        he[heuristics.py]
        bfs[bfs.py]
        ucs[ucs.py]
        gr[greedy.py]
        as[astar.py]
        rio[results_io.py]
        cmp[comparison.py]
    end
    tasks --> dv
    tasks --> t1
    tasks --> cmp
    ref --> sm
    ref --> t1
    dv --> sm
    dv --> t1
    sm --> t1
    dv --> sc
    sm --> sc
    dv --> he
    dv --> cmp
    sc --> bfs
    sc --> ucs
    sc --> gr
    sc --> as
    he --> gr
    he --> as
    bfs --> cmp
    ucs --> cmp
    gr --> cmp
    as --> cmp
    rio --> cmp
    cmp --> out
```

Notes on the diagram:

- `tasks.csv` is read through `load_tasks` in `data_validation.py`. `test_phase1.py` and `comparison.py` also name the file directly.

